# SYNTHETIC fixture, and it is the emitter's own output rather than a
# hand-written file: prober/src/emit.rs's emit_header was called once, then
# emit_endpoint once for data.kkg.kadaster.nl/query, and emit_footer was NOT
# called. That absence is the whole fixture. It cannot be a captured sweep,
# because producing one live would mean shipping a prober that dies halfway
# through a run.
#
# This is exactly the file a crashed prober leaves on disk, and exactly what
# web/load_run.py loads from it: the last statement here is a chunk's
# sw:completedEndpoint terminator, so the loader's cut-back to the last
# terminator takes nothing off. A run that died mid-chunk instead would have
# its unterminated tail dropped by that cut, so every chunk that reaches the
# store is a whole one and the run still carries no sw:finalised. Either way
# the store holds what is below.
#
# The inputs handed to the emitter: the eight metrics of prober/metrics.toml
# against one endpoint, all measured (max_cost "expensive", so nothing is
# declined), sw:metric:availability as Verdict::Indeterminate and the rest as
# the real 16:00 sweep in run-with-samples.nq graded them, one
# sw:declarationsRead of true, and a two-value class sample whose values are
# two of that sweep's own 59. concurrency 4, metric revision
# fnv1a64:5988db00fe7498fd, run 2026-08-23T04:00:00Z.
#
# Two of those inputs are chosen so that "whose facts is the page showing" is
# answerable by value rather than by trust, the same way run-two-sweeps.nq's
# one hand edit is: availability reads "indeterminate" here and "verified" in
# the 16:00 sweep, and this sample holds 2 classes where that sweep's holds
# 59.
#
# 67 quads, one run graph (urn:sparqlwatch:run:2026-08-23T04:00:00Z), one
# endpoint. It carries sw:emission and one sw:completedEndpoint and NO
# sw:finalised, which is the shape the read tier has to be able to talk
# about: a run in the store that did not finish.
#
# Loaded beside run-with-samples.nq (the real 2026-08-22T16:00:00Z sweep of
# three endpoints) it produces both of the two conditions at once, on two
# different endpoints of one store. For kadaster, whose chunk this run did
# write, the facts shown come from a run that did not finish. For
# qlever.dev/api/osm-planet, which this run never reached, the facts shown
# come from the finished-looking 16:00 sweep and the newest run in the store
# is one that did not finish and carries no sw:completedEndpoint naming it.
# The second is the case a per-endpoint query cannot see on its own, and it
# is the one a crash produces for every endpoint after the one it died on.
